As Benjamin Franklin's reputation as a leading intellectual and statesman grew, so did the demand for his time and attention. He found himself inundated with requests for favors, advice, and assistance from a wide range of people, from fellow politicians to aspiring writers and scientists.

To manage this deluge of requests, Franklin devised a system that would become famous in its own right: the "letter-box." This was a simple wooden box, about the size of a small chest, that Franklin installed in his study. It had a slot in the top, through which letters could be deposited, and a drawer inside, where Franklin could sort and respond to them.

The letter-box was a stroke of genius. It allowed Franklin to triage his correspondence, separating urgent matters from less pressing ones, and ensuring that he never missed an important request. It also gave him a measure of control over his time, allowing him to respond to letters at his convenience rather than being constantly interrupted by callers.

But the letter-box was more than just a practical tool. It was also a symbol of Franklin's commitment to efficiency and organization. He saw it as a way to embody the values of thrift, simplicity, and self-discipline that he had espoused throughout his life.

In fact, Franklin's letter-box became something of a legend in its own time. Other prominent figures, such as Thomas Jefferson and James Madison, adopted similar systems, and the idea of a "letter-box" became a byword for efficiency and good management.

